  "forensics-reverse-image": {
    title: "Steganography – Reverse Shell Hidden in a PNG Image",
    content: `
## 1. Get a .py file with which you can set up a reverse shell --> https://www.revshells.com (recommended shell: "Python3 windows" with cmd/powershell)

I developed the following file:

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_014.png)

## 2. Download a .png image and store the reverse shell inside it

Now it's time to choose an image to embed it in. In the end I will use this one:

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_003.png)

To embed the reverse shell I will use stegano (to install it, pip3 install stegano):

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_001.png)

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_004.png)

## 3. Transfer the image to Windows.

We use python3 -m http.server 8000 to transfer the image to Windows.

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_013.png)

Now we will access it from Linux using the browser.

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_015.png)

We download the image; for now I have it on the desktop.

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_010.png)

We can move it to Tmp to leave no trace and make it more realistic

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_002.png)

## 4. Create a Python file that downloads the file you have inside the image and then runs it.

I designed the following script:

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_011.png)

I also moved this file to the Tmp folder

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_005.png)

## 5. Open the port on our Kali machine with the nc command

We open the port on Kali with nc

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_008.png)

## 6. Run the Python script we created and watch the reverse shell happen.

We run the file on Windows

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_006.png)

And we verify that the reverse shell was established

![](/writeups/digital-forensics/revimg_009.png)

### Conclusion

In this exercise, we demonstrated how to embed a reverse shell in a PNG image and run the malicious code on a Windows system:

Obtaining the Reverse Shell: A Python3 reverse shell for Windows was obtained from revshells.com.

Embedding in an Image: Using the stegano library, the reverse shell was hidden inside a PNG image, allowing its transfer without raising suspicion.

Transfer to the Windows system: A local HTTP server on Linux was used to pass the image to Windows, where it was saved on the desktop.

Running the code on Windows: A Python script was created to extract and run the reverse shell from the image on the Windows system.

Opening the Port on Kali: A port was opened on Kali Linux with nc to receive the reverse shell connection.

Establishing the Reverse Shell: When the script was run, access to the Windows system was achieved through the reverse connection.

### Key Lessons:

Steganography can hide malicious code in harmless files.

Security must be prioritized to protect against this type of attack technique.
`,
  },
